Inline SVG stands in wikitext with plain names. A tag name reaches only as far as
[a-zA-Z0-9$.-], so a colon ends it and refuses the tag, and namespaced markup travels instead as
content declaring its own type.

<svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" width="100" height="100">
<metadata>a metadata element</metadata>
<title>a title</title>
<desc>a description</desc>
<g transform="translate(10,10)">
<circle cx="20" cy="20" r="8"/>
<text x="4" y="40">a text element</text>
<animate attributeName="opacity" from="0" to="1" dur="1s"/>
</g>
<madeup-svg-element/>
<madeup-svg-pair>an element the svg family does not name</madeup-svg-pair>
</svg>

<madeuptag>an element nobody recognises, whose name TiddlyWiki accepts</madeuptag>

<madeuptag/>

A typed block hands its content to the grammar that content names. The XML grammar gives a
prefix, its separator and its local name a scope each.

$$$image/svg+xml
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xl="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" version="1.1" viewBox="34 107 510 317" width="510pt" height="317pt"><metadata xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><dc:date>2012-05-10 07:32Z</dc:date><!
</metadata></svg>

<svg
  width="100"
  height="100">
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" />
  <font-face-name name="x" />
</svg>

<math
  display="block">
  <mspace width="1em" />
</math>
$$$
